 {"id": "TJ-GAP-085", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] A verdict names the rule id but not which FILE decided it: expose rule resolution provenance so a shadowing/override cannot hide (TJ-GAP-051 class)", "description": "The engine returns a quotable rule_id and reason — the strongest property of this firewall — but not the identity of the layer that won: engine builtin vs shipped YAML mirror vs user rule dir vs rule pack. That gap already cost this project a wave: TJ-GAP-051 (complete) records a shipped YAML mirror being loaded as USER rules and weakening 12 engine verdicts, and the same shadowing class was re-hit during the TJ-GAP-053 wave with a stale installed copy at ~/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d/00-builtins.yaml. Today the user-visible remedy is a documented same-id override that can LOWER a rule to warn — a legitimate feature — with nothing in the verdict or any log recording that a builtin was downgraded, so an operator cannot see the downgrade without reading the rules tree by hand. The review's observability lens flagged this as the one tamper-adjacent gap in an otherwise attributable design.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The verdict (and/or a documented debug surface) states which rule file/source decided, its priority, and whether it overrode a builtin of the same id. 2) A same-id override that lowers a builtin (e.g. block -> warn) is reported at decision time, not silently applied: the reason string names the overriding file. 3) A CLI surface replays a decision in one line for a given command (the bridge can already do this — surface it as a verb/flag so an operator does not have to pipe JSON by hand; cf. Codex's `sandbox` debug). 4) Tests pin the shadowing case: a stale/duplicate builtin copy in the user dir must be visible in the verdict, and a downgrade must be reported. 5) Documentation states the resolution order and how to detect a shadowing copy in one command.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-086", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Argument-size ceiling is unbounded and linear: 8 KB = 22 ms, 20 KB = 52 ms, no documented policy or guard", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone (n=200 each): p50 21.8 ms for an 8 KB argument and 51.7 ms for a 20 KB argument, against 2.2 ms for ordinary commands — cost grows linearly with argument size, so a host that pipes whole files through the firewall pays seconds per call. TJ-DF-024 (complete) fixed the acute case (one 8 KB argument cost ~7.5 s of CPU inside intercept(); now 22 ms), but nothing states a policy or pins the behaviour: there is no documented maximum, no guard against the size that makes a host stall, and no benchmark in CI. The report's framing: fine for a shell command, a real ceiling for file-sized input.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A stated policy: either an enforced/documented maximum argument size with an explicit, clear refusal above it, or a documented linear-cost budget with the measured curve. 2) A committed benchmark (small, bounded, runnable on a laptop) that reports p50/p99 for a small, an 8 KB, and a 20 KB argument, plus a ceiling assertion so a regression beyond the pinned numbers fails. 3) The policy is visible where an integrator reads it (README/bridge docs), not only in a test. 4) Regression coverage for the TJ-DF-024 class stays green under the new guard.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-087", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] install.sh resolves the checkout from $PWD and reports the environment dishonestly: invoked by absolute path it installs nothing, and it never tells the operator which containment tier they got", "description": "Verified twice on 2026-09-27: `HOME=<scratch> /path/to/repo/install.sh` run from another directory exits non-zero with 'no local checkout detected ... Supported install: run ./install.sh from a repository checkout' even though the script is sitting inside the checkout — the installer resolves ../ from $PWD instead of BASH_SOURCE, so the natural invocation (absolute path, from anywhere) silently installs nothing. Second half, same theme: the installer never checks or reports what the host can actually enforce. The project's own scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py prints an excellent, actionable remediation for the DEGRADED case ('install newuidmap/newgidmap plus /etc/subuid|/etc/subgid ranges for the calling user', or the root sysctl), but nothing runs it at install time — so an operator finishes a successful install believing they have filesystem containment when on Ubuntu they do not.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) install.sh locates its own checkout (BASH_SOURCE-based) and works when invoked by absolute path from any cwd; the failure message, when a checkout genuinely is absent, says which path it looked for. 2) A post-install capability check runs the pidns + fs-isolation probes and prints, in the install summary, which containment tier the host will actually get — with the remediation text when DEGRADED. 3) The check is advisory (never blocks a successful install) unless the user explicitly demands a tier, in which case it fails closed. 4) Tests: absolute-path invocation from a foreign cwd installs correctly; a DEGRADED host's install output contains the tier line and the remediation hint.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
-{"id": "TJ-DF-038", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Missing `uname` on PATH is diagnosed as 'this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' — a Linux host told it is not Linux", "description": "Verified 2026-09-27 by running the installed CLI with a deliberately crippled PATH: the wrapper's preflight does `if [ \"$(uname -s)\" != \"Linux\" ]` and, when `uname` is absent, the command substitution is empty, so the wrapper exits 2 printing 'terminal-jail: this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' (plus a stray 'line 452: uname: command not found' from the shell). The operator is sent to investigate their kernel when the real problem is a stripped PATH (containers, minimal images, sandboxed exec wrappers). Found while reviewing; the bwrap-absent probe had to be rebuilt with a fuller PATH to get an uncontaminated result, which is exactly the confusion a user would hit.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The preflight distinguishes 'uname is not on PATH' from 'this is not Linux' and says so, naming the missing tool (or reads /proc/version as a fallback so the check cannot be defeated by a thin PATH). 2) Same treatment for the other preflight binaries it depends on, at minimum unshare (already named) — a missing dependency must never be reported as a platform limitation. 3) A test runs the CLI with a PATH containing only the minimum, asserting the diagnostic names the missing tool and that the exit code still fails closed.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
+{"id": "TJ-DF-038", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "complete", "title": "[P3] Missing `uname` on PATH is diagnosed as 'this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' — a Linux host told it is not Linux", "description": "Verified 2026-09-27 by running the installed CLI with a deliberately crippled PATH: the wrapper's preflight does `if [ \"$(uname -s)\" != \"Linux\" ]` and, when `uname` is absent, the command substitution is empty, so the wrapper exits 2 printing 'terminal-jail: this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' (plus a stray 'line 452: uname: command not found' from the shell). The operator is sent to investigate their kernel when the real problem is a stripped PATH (containers, minimal images, sandboxed exec wrappers). Found while reviewing; the bwrap-absent probe had to be rebuilt with a fuller PATH to get an uncontaminated result, which is exactly the confusion a user would hit.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The preflight distinguishes 'uname is not on PATH' from 'this is not Linux' and says so, naming the missing tool (or reads /proc/version as a fallback so the check cannot be defeated by a thin PATH). 2) Same treatment for the other preflight binaries it depends on, at minimum unshare (already named) — a missing dependency must never be reported as a platform limitation. 3) A test runs the CLI with a PATH containing only the minimum, asserting the diagnostic names the missing tool and that the exit code still fails closed.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "cd31f7b", "completed_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "worker_summary": "Preflight: uname-absent falls back to /proc/sys/kernel/ostype then /proc/version before claiming non-Linux; if neither verifies, names uname as missing (install coreutils) and exits 2 fail-closed. 8 tests added (plugin/test_standalone_cli.py), full plugin suite 1454 passed / 7 skipped. Live probe PATH=/tmp/bare (no uname): help renders normally on this Linux host.", "guard_result": "Tier1 DEGRADED PASS (secrets+static_analysis clean; lint/tests skipped, nothing staged at post-commit guard)"}
 {"id": "JSONL-NORM-002", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Board schema drift: `priority` is not type-stable (P1 strings + bare ints) and 7 rows still use the legacy `completed` status next to 175 `complete`", "description": "Measured on this board 2026-09-27 (209 rows, last line per id wins): priority values are mixed types — 72 \"P1\" strings alongside 9 bare 1 ints, likewise P2 (72 strings / 7 ints), P3 (35 strings), P0 (11 strings); status uses both `complete` (175) and the legacy synonym `completed` (7). Related: 183 of 209 rows have no `type` field at all while 25 set \"task\", and `created_by` is null on 176 rows. Any count, sort or filter written against these fields is one drifted row away from being wrong, and the project's own recurring work is exactly counting and filtering rows (PM/releng/QA lanes).", "acceptance_criteria": "1) `priority` is one type everywhere (string, normalized to P0-P3) and `status` is one vocabulary (`complete`), with `completed` migrated. 2) A guard/probe (extend scripts/board_id_guard.py or a sibling) FAILS on a type-inconsistent priority or an unknown status, so the drift cannot return. 3) The migration is applied to tasks.jsonl in a single pass, with the census (row count + unique ids) printed before and after and quoted in the closing commit. 4) Counts quoted elsewhere (README/board summaries) are re-derived after the migration.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "QA-TERMINAL-JAIL-12", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Skip marker contradicts the host probe: a test skips as HOST-DEGRADED-PIDNS while scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py classifies this host FULL and the CLI demonstrably contains", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31: the suite reports 'SKIPPED [1] test_env_scrub.py:440 HOST-DEGRADED-PIDNS: host refused bare PID namespace creation', but scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py on the same host prints FULL (exit 0) and the live CLI creates a PID namespace on demand (bare mode: jail PID-ns inode 4026536818 vs host 4026531836, 5 visible PIDs vs 1129). So either the marker overstates a narrower precondition, or the classifier and the test disagree about what DEGRADED means. Both are bad in the same way: an honest-skip system that mislabels why it skipped is indistinguishable, in the output, from a real DEGRADED host — and this project's credibility rests on those skip names being true.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) The skip marker names the actual failed precondition (and the probe it agrees with), or the test runs on this host. Whichever is correct, the test's own message and the classifier agree. 2) A genuine DEGRADED host still skips with a truthful marker: prove it by exercising the classifier's two branches (e.g. a forced-degraded env or a documented fixture) and quoting both outputs. 3) The suite's skip output is cross-checked in CI (or by a QA cell) against the classifiers so a mislabelled skip is detected, not discovered by review. 4) No coverage is silently dropped: if the skipped assertion IS runnable here, it runs.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "INT-CI-2", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] '1437 tests' is a count, not a coverage claim: no pytest-cov in the dev group, so CI cannot trend what the suite does not touch", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone: `uv sync --dev` installs pytest + ruff + pyyaml only — [dependency-groups] dev = [\"pytest>=8\", \"ruff==0.16.8\"] — and there is no coverage tooling anywhere in the install path, so the suite's size (1437 passed / 7 skipped in 46 s, verified) cannot be turned into a coverage number by anyone, including CI, whose jobs are test/lint/audit/drift-guards. For a security tool the untested branches are the interesting ones, and a project whose README leans on its test count should be able to say what those tests reach.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) pytest-cov is in the dev dependency group (no new runtime dependency). 2) A documented command produces a coverage number for plugin/terminal_jail, and the measured number is recorded in the repo (README development section and/or a CI artifact) with the command that produced it. 3) CI reports it (job or artifact) so it can trend, and a floor is set at or below the measured baseline so regressions are visible — the floor must be the measured value, not an aspirational target. 4) The honest caveats are stated where the number is published (what is excluded: tests themselves, platform-gated paths).", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
